# Contemplating Contemplation

For most of human history, illuminated ones of many titles have rediscovered the practice of contemplation. We are all familiar with it on some level. We stare out a window with a question hanging somewhere behind the eyes, turn an experience over while walking, pray on something, pull an old book from the shelf because a sentence we once passed over suddenly seems to matter. Sometimes a question follows us for years before we understand what we were really asking.

Here I would like to consider what contemplation actually is so we can become more deliberate about developing the faculty without strangling it under technique.

The human being is a layered organism. The body, nervous system, memory, emotion, and rational mind each carry their own priorities and influence us to varying degrees. Their relationship shapes our behavior and the world as it appears from inside us. In classic Hermetic recursive fashion, thought alters the interior landscape while that landscape influences which thoughts arise. A memory can tighten the chest before the rational mind has finished remembering it; an imagined future can alter the breath while nothing whatsoever has happened outside the imagination.

Meditation gives us greater control over attention and creates some space around these movements. Once that space exists, a curious thing becomes possible: instead of immediately being carried away by whatever appears, we can begin studying it. Contemplation grows from that shift. We gather scattered pieces of experience and slowly build internal models of the world, then allow those models to change as experience supplies better information.

The written word, though sacred, is not the Word itself. Written truth remains a representation of something that ultimately has to be apprehended. You can memorize a thousand pages about forgiveness and still discover, when the knife actually goes in, that you have no idea what forgiveness means. Study gives us inherited maps. Contemplation makes us walk the country, and once you have walked some of it yourself the maps become far more interesting.

Words and symbols matter because meaning lives in relationship. A single word can contain years of memory and bodily association. Ideas that seem unrelated may share some deeper configuration that the mind recognizes before language catches up. Contemplation gives that recognition enough room to become intelligible.

One of the oldest ways of approaching this territory might be called **stalking the self**.

To stalk yourself, cultivate what I call a **zero attitude**. Zero does not mean blankness or indifference. It is closer to the attitude you might bring to an unfamiliar animal whose behavior you genuinely wanted to understand. Curiosity gets the first move.

Carry some of the neutral emotional posture developed through meditation into ordinary life and watch what happens before the rational mind starts cleaning up the evidence. A person says something and your stomach tightens. You suddenly want to leave the room. Some absurdly small task develops the emotional gravity of an approaching execution. Pay attention to the sequence while it is still fresh.

The explanation can come later.

If you immediately condemn the reaction, defend it, or construct a philosophy around it, you have already disturbed what you were trying to observe. Give the animal enough room to behave naturally. There is also a recursive turn hidden inside the practice: every once in a while, while observing what is happening, notice that you are the one observing it. Anger may occupy most of the room and yet some faculty is still capable of seeing anger happen. Attention can become so identified with whatever captures it that we disappear into the experience and forget we are present at all.

You do not need to become a spiritual surveillance camera. Catch the movement when you catch it.

Once you begin watching this way, certain questions naturally start presenting themselves. Some keep returning. Some carry more charge than the material event seems to justify. Others sit quietly in the background and refuse to die.

Let curiosity guide you and immediacy constrain what you contemplate.

There are vast questions common to human life, but lived reality usually gives you a perfectly good place to begin. What is happening now? What keeps repeating? Where does your experience refuse to fit the explanation you have been given? Begin close enough to the ground that reality can answer you.

This also makes better use of knowledge.

Information gathered randomly tends to fragment. You can collect facts for years and end up with an attic full of interesting boxes and no idea where the screwdriver is. Knowledge acquired inside a structure already has relationships built into it, and knowledge organized around direct experience often roots itself even more deeply because the question gives the information somewhere to live.

That is why I like history and story as trunks for learning. Once you understand the world in which something arose, details stop floating around as isolated facts. They begin attaching themselves to causes, people, consequences, and one another.

The same principle applies inwardly. A journal lets experiences that occurred weeks apart sit beside each other long enough for their relationships to become visible. Catch a charged moment close to when it happens and record what you can see from inside the state. Return later when the charge has shifted. Something that appeared perfectly obvious Tuesday afternoon may look very strange Friday morning.

This accumulation is where the deeper geometry begins to show itself.

Information and thought have geometry. Every concept has elements, configurations, and constraints, and each of those may contain another conceptual structure nested within it. Their relationships form patterns larger than any individual piece. Sometimes the mind recognizes the pattern before it can explain why.

You feel the fit and go looking.

Precise language becomes powerful here because a poorly understood word can conceal several different realities beneath one symbol. I learned something about this through forgiveness.

Many years ago, I had a difficult experience with a family member that caused me great material and spiritual harm. There was, and perhaps always will be, a part of me that wanted justice. Another part wanted protection. My rational mind could project possible scenarios far into the future and construct very persuasive arguments for why punishment of the perpetrator was necessary.

The social landscape at the time made pursuing justice effectively a zero-sum game. The sacrifices required would have damaged relationships I cared about and threatened my connection to my family, so I was forced to play the long game.

Hatred and anger are heavy things to carry, especially when the darker reaches of human nature begin entertaining homicidal revenge. That charge has to live somewhere while you are carrying it.

I began praying and asking God for his wisdom, his love, and his peace. I surrendered the problem to the divine and asked for guidance in finding some way to forgive the person who had wronged me. I did not know what that would actually mean, so forgiveness became a question I carried with me.

This is what I think of as **passive contemplation**.

The practice is beautifully simple. Keep a question somewhere in the back of the mind and let life accumulate around it.

Forgiveness began appearing everywhere. Scripture opened one angle; my own body opened another. Other people’s relationships became material. Conversations I had forgotten suddenly mattered. The question stayed alive without demanding an immediate answer, and over time the thing I had been calling forgiveness started separating into distinct forms.

In words, I had forgiven the person.

In my body and nervous system, it was not even close.

Rationally I had chosen forgiveness because it seemed like the right thing to do, and because another path carried serious social consequences for people I cared about. Eventually I realized that several processes had been packed into one word.

The first I came to think of as **release**. You recognize the resentment, understand something of what it is doing to you, and put down the demand for revenge. Release is unilateral. The person who harmed you does not have to cooperate for you to stop allowing the injury to govern your interior life.

Trust has its own clock.

There is also **forbearance**, where some form of peace or continued relationship remains possible while repair is incomplete. The nervous system learns from repeated experience, and if the old pattern returns, forbearance can be withdrawn.

**Exoneration** reaches the relationship itself. It requires repentance expressed through restitution and a genuine change in the pattern of behavior that caused the injury.

That is not how my situation played out. Predictably, the whole thing crashed and burned after fifteen years. The contemplation still helped me find enough peace to survive the waiting while the people who had wronged me eventually stepped on the rake they had left out.

What changed was my ability to see the internal structure of the problem. Rational forgiveness, bodily trust, and genuine repair had been stacked beneath one word, and I had been demanding that every layer of myself obey the same decision at the same speed.

Once a question reaches beyond your own experience, contemplation naturally opens into immersion. There are things another person has already spent forty years thinking about, and sometimes wisdom consists in having enough humility to go find out what they discovered.

Scripture is central to this in my own practice. The writings of old masters and the accumulated record of other human beings trying to understand the same strange world extend contemplation beyond what one lifetime could discover alone. I like to approach a thinker through the world that produced the thought. Learn enough of the history that it becomes the trunk. The details will find their places.

A person’s ideas become more intelligible once you can feel the circumstances around them. What did reality look like from where they were standing? What pressures shaped the question they were trying to answer?

Sit with someone long enough, even across centuries, and the marble bust starts becoming human.

Then turn the same curiosity back upon yourself.

Your personal history is also a story, and the story remains active in the present. An event becomes an interpretation; eventually the interpretation begins predicting what kind of person you are and what kind of world you inhabit. Somewhere along the way *this happened to me* can quietly become *this is who I am*.

Self-importance can enter through the same machinery. Injured pride may become an elaborate theory of justice. The need to be right can dress itself in excellent logic and stroll straight through the front door. The zero attitude allows you to notice this without turning self-observation into self-prosecution.

Once you begin suspecting that personal history is shaping perception, externalizing the story becomes useful because what remained invisible inside the loop can finally assume a form you can inspect.

Writing does this directly. An emotionally charged idea may circulate between memory and sensation until repetition itself begins to feel like evidence. Put it on the page and the sentence sits still long enough to be examined from another position. You can return tomorrow and discover that the thing which felt like revelation at midnight now appears to have been written by a raccoon trapped in a liquor cabinet.

Serious conversation can perform the same function because your private model encounters another mind. Our era has added a strange instrument to the old study: an AI model, properly tuned, allows an internal state to be externalized almost as it happens while preserving continuity across observations that might otherwise disappear back into the stream.

Art gets somewhere stranger. Creative play can loosen the ordinary arrangement of mind enough for something to emerge before the rational voice knows exactly what it is saying. You finish a song or poem and discover that there was information inside it you never consciously decided to put there.

Once these external traces accumulate across time, another property of thought becomes easier to see: **trajectory**.

An interior movement goes somewhere.

The first emotional tone does not necessarily reveal its destination. A thought that initially feels peaceful may slowly tighten the whole interior world around itself. Something painful can unfold into clarity once the first resistance has passed. Discernment develops partly through learning to watch what kind of state grows from following a movement over time.

For me this is inseparable from prayer. I ask God for wisdom because the rational mind is extraordinarily capable of constructing explanations for things some deeper part of me has already decided it wants.

Watching trajectory also reveals a simple difference between contemplation and one of its shadows: some thought does not actually go anywhere.

That is rumination.

The question has stopped opening, yet the mind keeps chewing. The same material returns with the same charge, memory feeding emotion and emotion summoning the memory back for another lap. There may be enormous intellectual activity inside this loop while almost nothing is being learned.

Once you recognize that state, meditation becomes useful again.

Leave language for a while. Take a deep breath and put attention somewhere simpler: breath, bodily sensation, prayer, mantra, silence. Let the nervous system settle while the unresolved question remains quietly bookmarked.

Nothing says contemplation requires continuous conscious thought.

Sometimes the missing piece simply has not arrived yet. Life keeps happening while you are doing something else, and days later an observation may fall into place that changes the whole geometry. Passive contemplation leaves room for this. The question has gone quiet without being abandoned.

There is another way contemplation can lose the thread: thinking itself is pleasurable.

A clever rational mind can spend forty minutes constructing a metaphysical cathedral around the fact that somebody hurt its feelings.

There may be excellent architecture in there. Find the hurt too.

Pontification begins when complexity itself starts supplying the reward. Language continues multiplying while the object under contemplation has stopped changing. At that point your enjoyment of thinking becomes part of the material being stalked.

Abstraction gives this tendency a great deal more room.

As a concept moves farther from material reality, the world corrects us less immediately and interpretation gains freedom. More of the thinker can leak into the thought unnoticed. Intelligence can make the problem worse simply by supplying better tools for rationalization.

Concrete reality can be wonderfully rude. Build something badly and it will often tell you.

At higher altitude the feedback becomes obscure. Theology can absorb an enormous amount of interpretation. Politics can swallow contradiction for breakfast. A sufficiently flexible metaphysics can protect itself from almost anything if the thinker keeps rebuilding the walls.

So know your altitude.

The more abstract the subject becomes, the more deliberately include your relationship to the idea among the things under observation. Notice where identity has become tangled with the conclusion and keep looking for places where material reality can still answer back.

Hold your maps strongly enough to use them and lightly enough that reality can still redraw them.

Eventually some questions reach a point where another hour of thought contributes less than a simple action. You notice a strange repulsion toward an ordinary task, stalk it long enough to understand something of the charge, then do the thing. What happens supplies information the interior model could not.

This is where the four pillars begin weaving into one another almost by themselves. Meditation has already rescued contemplation from rumination. The body has repeatedly supplied evidence that words alone could not. Another human being can expose an angle your private model cannot occupy.

Integration is the capstone.

There is far more to contemplation than can fit in a primer like this. Good. There should be.

Read the old books. Sit with people who have walked farther than you. Spend time alone when a question asks for silence, then carry it back into ordinary life where the world can have its say.

Let curiosity choose the question and lived reality keep your feet on the ground.

Some questions need an answer now. Others deserve to sit quietly beside you for a while, like an old teacher drinking tea in the corner of the room, waiting for you to notice what has been obvious for years.
